cartridge "RINGSIDE BOXING/COMPUTE! GAZETTE/1987"

' RINGSIDE BOXING
' A ColecoVision game based on "Ringside Boxing", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' March 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, 4,782 bytes started with
' SYS 2485; no author named on the disk). Made for Amy Studio by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/boxing.dis, and timed in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a side view of a ring with a crowd, ropes and
' posts, two boxers in headgear as smooth sprites (PURPLE and GREEN, as on
' the C64), sound effects and a title tune.
'
' From the original (orig/boxing.dis), in 60 Hz frames:
' - two boxers face each other; the stick toward the other steps in, any
'   other direction steps back, one pixel every other frame each (the
'   C64's IRQ moved purple and green on alternate interrupts), never
'   closer than a set distance;
' - the button with up + toward throws a jab (costs 2 energy, takes 5 and
'   scores 5), with down + toward a hook to the body (costs 5, takes 10,
'   scores 10), alone (or with any other direction) raises the guard for
'   about 0.8 s; the button must be let go between moves;
' - a punch lands only on a boxer in reach and not punching or guarding;
' - energy starts at 198 (shown as 99%), never drops below 21, comes back
'   1 point every 109 frames up to 197, and 10 points between rounds;
' - a boxer hit while under 50 energy goes down for a count of
'   25 - energy / 2; a count of 10 or more is a knockout; while he is down
'   the other backs away to his corner;
' - three rounds of "3:00" on the clock, one clock second every 19 frames
'   (about 57 s a round); after the third round the higher score wins.
'
' Liberties: a punch is open to a counter once its moment has passed (the
' C64's poses lasted a whole pass of its main loop, so mashing the button
' was safe there); a punch into a guard costs the puncher 3 more energy;
' a short recovery (6 frames) after every move; a tie on points is a draw
' (the C64 gave it to purple); one player against a computer GREEN at two
' skills (EASY sees you 16 frames late, HARD 10), besides the original
' two-player game; the C64's three-loop guard and loop-timed punches
' became 48- and 16-frame moves with the hit tested at the punch's eighth
' frame; hits by distance (the C64 tested sprite collisions); the end
' message, then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 PURPLE (left, facing right), controller 2 GREEN.
' One player: controller 1 plays PURPLE. On the title, up and down choose
' a line, left and right change it, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

